A||||11||86SE||Rear Calipers - Piston is EXTREMELY hard to push in....||||11||
Z||000000||86SE||09-30-2000||03:25 PM||||I went to change my rear brake pads Wednesday.  Got the old ones off no problem.  I go to push the piston back into its bore and it will no move a bit!  I have tried everything, from a C-clamp with a cheater bar, to a big hammer and an aluminum pipe.  It does not go in!<P>Come to think of it, I dont even think the brakes in the rear even work.  They seem like they don't even grab when I slam on the brakes.<P>Anyone have new calipers they want to sell??  or just any that work??<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||11
Z||000001||StuGood||09-30-2000||04:04 PM||||Take off the e-brake lever, and use a big socket to cover the e-brake screw while you compress the piston with a C-clamp. <P>Next, try cracking open the bleeder.  If that allows you to compress the caliper piston, I would bet you need to replace the flexible brake hose.  If the caliper itself is locked, just bite the bullet and get a rebuilt caliper from your local parts purveyor.  They should still be available.  Be prepared to pay more core charge than you're used to.<P><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 09-30-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000002||Rodrv6||09-30-2000||04:10 PM||||REAR brakes?  You must turn the piston in order to retract it due to the parking brake mechanism in the piston.  Most parts stores sell a special tool that fits in the notches in the face of the piston so you can turn it with a 3/8 drive wrench.  It may be possible to insert the ends of a pair of needle nose pliers in there and do the same thing.  The only way to just push them in is to remove the parking brake lever on the back of the caliper so that the actuator screw can turn as the piston retracts, but just turning the piston is probably easier.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE<p>[This message has been edited by Rodrv6 (edited 09-30-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000003||2xsess||09-30-2000||05:41 PM||||Rod is right, I found out the hard way too. I pushed and clamped and beat on one of my rear calipers for hours once, the tool is cheap and makes it alot easier!!!!<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-30-2000||08:17 PM||||The procedure, just to get it all in one message, is:<P>remove e brake arm.<BR>cover e brake actuator with large socket. (Nothing should touch e brake "bolt")<BR>press in piston with large C clamp.<P>With the e brake lever off they should retract easily. This is the recomended way to do this on all but 88 fiero.<P>If the above procedure doesn't work the caliper needs to be rebuilt/replaced.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||EdsB52||09-30-2000||09:18 PM||||My favorite way is to turn the caliper in with a special block tool. One thing to remember is that you must turn the left caliper (drivers side) counterclockwise, as it has left-handed threads, and the right caliper clockwise.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||10-01-2000||02:47 PM||||If all else fails Autozone has rebuilt rear calipers for pre-88s for $32.00 with an $80.00 core charge.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||10-01-2000||09:06 PM||||You don't need a special tool, just do it like Ogre says. You have to remove the e-brake arm so that it turns free as you compress the piston.||||reg||1
Z||000008||WisconsinGT||10-01-2000||09:45 PM||||Orge, what is the procedure for the 88's? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000009||skitime||10-01-2000||10:14 PM||||On my 88 I just remove the e brake arm and press the piston in like normal calibers. I did it today after installing Cavalier rotors from Autozone for 13.99 each.||||reg||1
Z||000010||EdsB52||10-02-2000||12:58 AM||||I still prefer my way of creating clearance for new pads, by simply turning the piston.||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||10-02-2000||08:39 PM||||According to the haynes book 88's you rotate the pistons. One turns CCW the other CW. I fogot which is which. Those calipers are totally differant.||||reg||1
